WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.669
Authorization answers the question, what is this identity allowed to do.

2
00:00:04.669 --> 00:00:05.965
In ASP.

3
00:00:05.965 --> 00:00:12.644
NET Core, authorization can be role-based, claim-based, policy-based, resource-based, or a combination.

4
00:00:12.644 --> 00:00:15.562
The right model depends on the business rule.

5
00:00:15.562 --> 00:00:21.268
A good access-control design should be easy to explain and easy to locate during review.

6
00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:29.827
Roles can be useful when they map to real responsibilities, such as an administrator, support operator, approver, or tenant manager.

7
00:00:29.827 --> 00:00:38.646
They become too coarse when one role is expected to capture every record-level, tenant-level, workflow-level, and action-level decision.

8
00:00:38.646 --> 00:00:43.963
Broad roles are easy to apply but hard to reason about when the application grows.

9
00:00:43.963 --> 00:00:52.457
Claims describe attributes about the identity, such as an organization, tenant relationship, assurance level, or permission marker.

10
00:00:52.457 --> 00:00:57.644
Claims are useful inputs to decisions, but they should be interpreted carefully.

11
00:00:57.644 --> 00:01:07.176
A claim usually tells the application something about the caller; it does not automatically prove access to every resource referenced by a request.

12
00:01:07.176 --> 00:01:11.196
Policies let teams define reusable authorization requirements.

13
00:01:11.196 --> 00:01:19.236
They can combine roles, claims, authentication scheme expectations, custom checks, and other requirements into a named rule.

14
00:01:19.236 --> 00:01:32.075
Policies are helpful because endpoint declarations become more readable and because reviewers can inspect the policy implementation instead of chasing repeated logic across controllers and handlers.

15
00:01:32.075 --> 00:01:37.587
Resource-based authorization is important when access depends on the specific object.

16
00:01:37.587 --> 00:01:41.477
A user may be allowed to view reports, but not every report.

17
00:01:41.477 --> 00:01:47.313
A tenant administrator may approve one tenant's request, but not another tenant's request.

18
00:01:47.313 --> 00:01:57.752
Resource checks may require loading the resource, verifying ownership, checking workflow state, and then making an authorization decision close to the operation.

19
00:01:57.752 --> 00:02:00.605
Access rules must be enforced on the server.

20
00:02:00.605 --> 00:02:08.840
Hiding a button, disabling a menu item, or omitting a link can improve the user experience, but it is not the security control.

21
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:21.160
MVC actions, Razor Pages, controllers, APIs, Minimal APIs, background/admin surfaces, and operational endpoints should have server-side protection that matches the sensitivity of the action.
